# The Art of Fugue, BWV 1080: Contrapunctus 13, a 3, rectus (J. S. Bach)
# The three-voice mirror fugue, in its upright form. In the 1751 print
# and modern editions it is Contrapunctus 13 (XIII): rectus and inversus.
# Mutopia files it as "Contrapunctus XVI", apparently following an older
# edition's numbering; I could not confirm which. D minor, 2/2, 71 bars.
#
# Bars: the score opens with a quarter-note pickup, and the MIDI counts
# its bars from that pickup. So bar N here is the score's bar N shifted
# a quarter earlier: it starts on the last beat of score bar N-1. Every
# entry begins with an upbeat, which lands on the first beat of the bar
# named here, so the numbers below match the score's bar numbers.
#
# Character: lively and good-humoured, almost a gigue in feel: the
# subject is a free, ornamented version of the main Art of Fugue theme
# (a leap of a fourth, an octave drop, then running triplets), with
# dotted figures throughout. The Mutopia edition writes the dotted
# rhythms assimilated to the triplets. Reviews on bach-cantatas.com
# describe Leonhardt as "lyrical" with "strong momentum", Gilbert as
# "clear, invigorating", Koroliov as fast. It should dance but not hurry.
#
# Tempo: half note = 60, about 2:25. 71 bars of 2/2 is 142 halves.
# Per-piece timings found: Albert Cano Smit (piano) 2:11 (half 65); the
# Nosrati recording booklet 2:10 (half 66); Hall's brass recording
# (Sauer arrangement) 2:16 (half 63). Played as a pair with the inversus
# (AOF3B) at the same tempo. I could not find per-piece timings for
# Leonhardt, Koroliov, Aimard, Hewitt, Moroney or the Emerson Quartet.
# 60 is a little steadier than those, so the triplets stay clear.
#
# Structure (subject entries from teoria.com's Art of Fugue analysis:
# subject alto, first answer bass, second answer soprano; bar numbers,
# later entries and keys are my own reading of the Mutopia score):
#   1-4    subject in the alto (mid), D minor: up a fourth, down an octave,
#          rising triplets. The alto is alone.
#   5-8    answer in the bass (bottom), inverted shape (falling triplets)
#   9-12   second answer in the soprano (top); trill in the bass at 11,
#          cadence on A at 12-13
#   13-19  episode: dotted arpeggio figures in sequence
#   20-23  inverted form in the alto, D minor; cadence in D minor at 23
#   23-26  falling sequence over quarter notes in the bass (D, G, C, F)
#   27-29  head in the soprano, E-flat major
#   29-32  subject form in the bass, B-flat major (soprano holds B-flat
#          30-32); cadence in B-flat at 32
#   33-36  inverted form in the soprano, B-flat
#   37-40  subject form in the alto, G minor, over a G pedal in the bass
#          (38-41)
#   41-47  episode toward the dominant of D minor
#   48-51  inverted form in the alto, D minor
#   52-58  rising sequence, soprano and alto in imitation one bar apart
#   59     fermata on a diminished seventh chord (C sharp, G, B-flat)
#   60-61  running triplets, transition
#   62-65  subject form in the bass, D minor: the last entry in the bass
#   66-69  inverted form in the soprano, D minor, over an A pedal in the
#          bass (66-68)
#   69-71  final cadence onto D major (F sharp in the alto), fermata

tempo 60 2             # half note, see above
voice mid 0            # the alto has as many entries as the outer voices

dyn 1 mp               # bars 1-12: exposition
lead 5-8 bottom        # bars 5-8: answer in the bass (the soprano has not entered,
                       # so the alto is the top line here)
lead 9-12 top          # bars 9-12: second answer in the soprano

dyn 13 mf              # bars 13-26: after the cadence on A, episode and the alto entry
lead 20-23 mid         # bars 20-23: inverted form in the alto

dyn 27 f               # bars 27-40: the relative major, entries in E-flat, B-flat, G minor
lead 27-28 top         # bars 27-28: head in the soprano, E-flat
lead 29-32 bottom      # bars 29-32: subject form in the bass, B-flat
lead 33-36 top         # bars 33-36: inverted form in the soprano
lead 37-40 mid         # bars 37-40: subject form in the alto, over the G pedal

dyn 41 mp              # bars 41-47: after the G minor entry, the episode back toward D minor
dyn 48 mf              # bars 48-51: the alto brings the tonic back
lead 48-49 mid         # bars 48-49: inverted form in the alto (the soprano dips
                       # into its range at 50, so stop there)

ramp 52-58 f           # bars 52-58: the rising sequence climbs to the fermata
broaden 59 0.7         # bar 59: the fermata on the diminished seventh

dyn 60 mf              # bars 60-61: after the fermata, the running transition
dyn 62 f               # bars 62-71: the last two entries, bass then soprano
lead 62-65 bottom      # bars 62-65: subject form in the bass
lead 66-69 top         # bars 66-69: inverted form in the soprano, over the A pedal

rit 2 0.7              # last 2 bars into the D major chord and its fermata
